{"cuisine/burundian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/burundian-cuisine","n":"Burundian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Burundi","intro":[["Burundi is situated in East Africa and has a territory full of mountains, savannas and agricultural fields, with forests in the surrounding rivers and waters. Agriculture is spread on 80% of the country's surface and it mainly includes coffee, tea, maize, beans and manioc. Burundi cuisine also includes beans, which are the staple of Burundi cooking, exotic fruits (mainly bananas), plantains, sweet potatoes, cassava, peas, maize, and cereals, like corn and wheat."],["The Burundian people usually eat homemade food, from homemade vessels also used for drinking, carrying water and storing grain. Food security remains a major problem in Burundi."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"burundian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burundian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/4/4d/Meal-options_in_Burundi.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"[https://augustinetours.com Augustine Tours]","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meal-options_in_Burundi.jpg"}},"cuisine/balochi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/balochi-cuisine","n":"Balochi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Pakistan","intro":[["Balochi cuisine is the food and cuisine of the Baloch people native to the Balochistan region spanning Afghanistan, Iran, and Pakistan, as well as diaspora communities worldwide. Baloch food has a regional variance in contrast to the many cuisines of Pakistan and Iran."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"balochi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balochi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ab/Balochi_Namkeen_Roast.JPG/500px-Balochi_Namkeen_Roast.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Miansari66","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balochi_Namkeen_Roast.JPG"}},"cuisine/ancient-egyptian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/ancient-egyptian-cuisine","n":"Ancient Egyptian cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Historical cuisine","intro":[["The cuisine of ancient Egypt encompasses the food and drink consumed by the ancient Egyptians throughout a span of over three thousand years, which retained many consistent traits until well into Greco-Roman times. The staples of the ancient Egyptian diet were bread and beer, which were supplemented with vegetables, legumes, fruits, meat, and seasonings prepared in a multitude of ways."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ancient-egyptian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Food Fit for Pharaohs: An Ancient Egyptian Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/food-fit-for-pharaohs-an-ancient-egyptian-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Michelle Berriedale-Johnson","note":"British Museum Press book of recipes built from ingredients and dishes known from ancient Egypt.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780714119298","suggested":true}]}],"cont":null,"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ae/Egyptian_harvest.jpg/500px-Egyptian_harvest.jpg","w":500,"h":439,"by":"Anonymous Egyptian tomb artist(s)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_harvest.jpg"}},"cuisine/soviet-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/soviet-cuisine","n":"Soviet cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Historical cuisine","intro":[["Soviet cuisine, the common cuisine of the Soviet Union, was formed by the integration of the various national cuisines of the Soviet Union, in the course of the formation of the Soviet people. It is characterized by a limited number of ingredients and simplified cooking. This type of cuisine was prevalent in  canteens everywhere in the Soviet Union. It became an integral part of household cuisine and was used in parallel with national dishes, particularly in large cities. Generally, Soviet cuisine was shaped by Soviet eating habits and a very limited availability of ingredients in most parts of the USSR. Most dishes were simplifications of French, Russian, Austro-Hungarian cuisines, and cuisines from other Eastern Bloc nations. Caucasian cuisines, particularly Georgian cuisine, contributed as well."],["To a significant extent it was reflected in and formed by The Book of Tasty and Healthy Food, first printed in 1939, following the directions of Anastas Mikoyan. This book included recipes for sophisticated meals that few Soviet citizens could afford to cook, as these recipes represented a utopia of the types of meals one might eat in the future. The book is an example of Soviet propaganda, which was in contrast with actual experience, in which the promises of abundance were fulfilled only for the chosen."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"soviet-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Mastering the Art of Soviet Cooking: A Memoir of Food and Longing","r